## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design a complete edge-CV pipeline: real-time monocular 3D pose with per-object velocity, occlusion robustness, and rapid on-device adaptation to unseen parts. Emphasizes practical data strategies and evaluation in an industrial setting.

## Key Concepts
- Real-time monocular 3D pose estimation
- Per-instance velocity prediction for planning
- On-device few-shot adaptation (5-shot)
- Occlusion handling and data fusion strategies

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-code: forward pass with adaptation trigger
function forward(frame, exemplars){
  let features = backbone(frame);
  let detections = detector(features);
  let poses = poseHead(features, detections);
  let vel = velocityHead(features, detections, previous);
  if (exemplars) adapt(poses, exemplars);
  return {detections, poses, vel};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you quantify robustness to occlusion in your evaluation?
- What ablation would you run to isolate the velocity head contribution?

## Why This Is Asked

This question probes the ability to design real-time 3D scene understanding from monocular video in crowded environments, focusing on dynamic occupancy rather than static poses. It tests architectural choices, data strategy, and on-device adaptation under latency constraints.

## Key Concepts

- Monocular 3D occupancy mapping in dynamic scenes
- Temporal fusion and uncertainty estimation
- On-device 5-shot adaptation for unseen objects and people
- Privacy-preserving synthetic data pipelines and domain randomization
- Edge GPU optimization and latency budgeting

## Code Example

```javascript
// Skeleton: simple occupancy loss combining BCE and temporal consistency
function occupancyLoss(logits, target, mask, prevLogits){
  // logits/target/mask shapes: [B, X, Y, Z]
  const bce = binaryCrossEntropy(logits, target, mask);
  const temporal = meanSquaredError(logits, prevLogits, mask);
  return bce + 0.1 * temporal;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you evaluate robustness to occlusions and rapid appearance changes?
- How would you extend to multiple RGB sensors and fuse viewpoints?

## Why This Is Asked

Assesses the ability to architect privacy-aware computer vision systems under strict edge constraints while balancing accuracy and privacy guarantees. Requires concrete architecture, differentiable privacy terms, and rigorous evaluation plans.

## Key Concepts

- Privacy-preserving CV and de-identification modules
- Differentiable privacy loss and threat-model-aware evaluation
- Edge/on-device inference and latency budgeting
- Trade-offs between detection accuracy and privacy guarantees

## Code Example

```javascript
// Lightweight total loss combining detection accuracy and privacy
function computeTotalLoss(detectionPred, privacyPred, targets) {
    const detectionLoss = computeDetectionLoss(detectionPred, targets);
    const privacyLoss = computePrivacyLoss(privacyPred, targets.sensitiveRegions);

    // Weighted combination with privacy budget constraint
    return detectionLoss + lambdaPrivacy * privacyLoss;
}

// Differentiable de-identification module
class PrivacyModule {
    obfuscate(features, sensitiveRegions) {
        // Apply learnable obfuscation to sensitive regions
        return features.map((feat, i) => 
            sensitiveRegions[i] ? this.anonymize(feat) : feat
        );
    }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical monocular depth reasoning and tracking in a simple inspection setting.

## Key Concepts
- Monocular depth proxy with known geometry
- Lightweight detectors, on-device adaptation
- Temporal tracking and data efficiency

## Code Example
```javascript
function projectTo3D(bbox, sheetDims, intrinsics) {
  // heuristic: depth proxy from vertical size and known sheet height
  const heightPx = bbox.height;
  const Z = (sheetDims.height * intrinsics.fy) / heightPx;
  return {X: 0, Y: 0, Z};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would occlusion or specular glare affect depth proxy? how to mitigate?
- How would you measure success and compare to a stereo baseline?

## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates the ability to design end-to-end computer vision systems that fuse perception with physics for deformable objects in cluttered environments, while also demonstrating expertise in efficient on-device adaptation.

## Key Concepts
- On-device few-shot adaptation
- 2.5D depth and surface normals representation
- Differentiable physics for stiffness estimation
- Grasp affordance learning with temporal consistency

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode: training loop for temporally-consistent affordance learning
for (batch of data) {
  const pred = model.forward(images);
  const affordanceLoss = computeBCE(pred.heatmap, target.heatmap);
  const depthLoss = computeL1(pred.depth, target.depth);
  const physicsLoss = computePhysicsConsistency(pred.stiffness, pred.depth);

  const totalLoss = affordanceLoss + depthLoss + physicsLoss;
  optimizer.step(totalLoss);
}
